/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Judges chapters 16-20
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("judges", [
{n:16, name:"Samson and Delilah", seal:{ref:"Judges 16:30",verse:"And Samson said, Let me die with the Philistines. And he bowed himself with all his might; and the house fell upon the lords, and upon all the people that were therein. So the dead which he slew at his death were more than they which he slew in his life."},
 facts:["Did you know Samson carried the gates of Gaza, posts and all, to the top of a hill before Hebron, a feat of strength the Philistines could never answer?", "Did you know Samson's seven locks of hair were the outward sign of his Nazirite vow, and his real failure began long before the razor touched his head?", "Did you know Samson killed more Philistines in his death than in his whole life, toppling Dagon's temple with a single final prayer?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Where did Samson go at the start of the chapter, where the Philistines laid wait for him?", options:["Gaza","Ashkelon","Ekron","Gath"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:1",verse:"Then went Samson to Gaza, and saw there an harlot, and went in unto her.",
   insight:"Gaza was a Philistine stronghold. Samson walked into the enemy's city alone, confident in the strength God had given him."},
  {q:"What did Samson do at midnight while the men of Gaza waited to kill him?", options:["He took the city gates, posts and bar, and carried them away","He set fire to their wheat fields","He slew a thousand of them with a jawbone","He blew a trumpet and called Israel to battle"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:3",verse:"And Samson lay till midnight, and arose at midnight, and took the doors of the gate of the city, and the two posts, and went away with them, bar and all, and put them upon his shoulders, and carried them up to the top of an hill that is before Hebron.",
   insight:"He did not just slip past them. He carried the gates themselves up a hill, showing them their walls could not hold him."},
  {q:"To which hill did Samson carry the gates of Gaza?", options:["The hill before Hebron","Mount Carmel","The hill of Moreh","Mount Zion"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:3",verse:"And Samson lay till midnight, and arose at midnight, and took the doors of the gate of the city, and the two posts, and went away with them, bar and all, and put them upon his shoulders, and carried them up to the top of an hill that is before Hebron.",
   insight:"Hebron lay far inland in Judah's hill country, so this was a long, heavy climb meant to display God's power over Philistine pride."},
  {q:"In what valley did Delilah live?", options:["The valley of Sorek","The valley of Jezreel","The valley of Eshcol","The valley of Achor"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:4",verse:"And it came to pass afterward, that he loved a woman in the valley of Sorek, whose name was Delilah.",
   insight:"Sorek lay in the border country between Israel and Philistia, a fitting place for a man who lived between two allegiances."},
  {q:"What did Delilah first ask Samson to tell her?", options:["Wherein his great strength lay","Who had taught him to fight","Where he had hidden the gates of Gaza","Why he never married a Hebrew wife"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:6",verse:"And Delilah said to Samson, Tell me, I pray thee, wherein thy great strength lieth, and wherewith thou mightest be bound to afflict thee.",
   insight:"Her question sounded tender but was bought and paid for. Samson's first mistake was answering her at all."},
  {q:"What did Samson claim would weaken him the first time?", options:["Being bound with seven green withs","Being bound with new ropes","Having his hair woven into a web","Having his locks shaved off"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:7",verse:"And Samson said unto her, If they bind me with seven green withs that were never dried, then shall I be weak, and be as another man.",
   insight:"He lied to Delilah, but he was really lying to himself, treating the source of his strength like a joke."},
  {q:"Who brought the seven green withs to bind Samson?", options:["The lords of the Philistines","Delilah's servants","Men of Gaza","The men of Timnath"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:8",verse:"Then the lords of the Philistines brought up to her seven green withs which had not been dried, and she bound him with them.",
   insight:"The Philistine lords paid Delilah for the secret, so from the start this was a paid betrayal, not a lovers' quarrel."},
  {q:"What did Samson claim would weaken him the second time?", options:["Being bound with new ropes that were never occupied","Being bound with seven green withs","Having his seven locks woven with the web","Losing his eyes"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:11",verse:"And he said unto her, If they bind me fast with new ropes that never were occupied, then shall I be weak, and be as another man.",
   insight:"He moved a step closer to the truth each time, handing Delilah more of his confidence with every lie."},
  {q:"What did Delilah do to Samson's hair while he slept the third time?", options:["She wove his seven locks with the web","She shaved his locks","She tied them to the beam","She poured oil on his head"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:14",verse:"And she fastened it with the pin, and said unto him, The Philistines be upon thee, Samson. And he awaked out of his sleep, and went away with the pin of the beam, and with the web.",
   insight:"Sleeping through such handling shows how safe he felt in her hands. Comfort had made him careless."},
  {q:"How did Delilah finally wear Samson down?", options:["She pressed him daily with her words and urged him","She wept only once and he gave in","She threatened to leave him","She told the Philistine lords his answer"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:16",verse:"And it came to pass, when she pressed him daily with her words, and urged him, so that his soul was vexed unto death;",
   insight:"It was not one plea but the daily wearing of nagging words. Small, repeated pressure undid what armies could not."},
  {q:"What was Samson's true secret, as he finally told Delilah?", options:["No razor had ever come upon his head, for he was a Nazarite to God from the womb","He drank a secret potion each morning","An angel guarded him in battle","He had been trained by the men of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:17",verse:"That he told her all his heart, and said unto her. There hath not come a razor upon mine head; for I have been a Nazarite unto God from my mother’s womb: if I be shaven, then my strength will go from me, and I shall become weak, and be like any other man.",
   insight:"The hair was only the sign. The real secret was his consecration to God, and he traded that consecration for a woman's flattery."},
  {q:"Who shaved off Samson's seven locks while he slept on Delilah's knees?", options:["A man she called for","Delilah herself","A Philistine soldier","The lord of Gaza"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:19",verse:"And she made him sleep upon her knees; and she called for a man, and she caused him to shave off the seven locks of his head; and she began to afflict him, and his strength went from him.",
   insight:"She lulled him to sleep first. Sin often soothes its victims before it strips them."},
  {q:"What tragic thing did Samson not know when he woke?", options:["That the LORD was departed from him","That Delilah had taken his sword","That the Philistines had surrounded the house","That his hair would never grow again"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:20",verse:"And she said, The Philistines be upon thee, Samson. And he awoke out of his sleep, and said, I will go out as at other times before, and shake myself. And he wist not that the LORD was departed from him.",
   insight:"He assumed God's strength would be there as always. This is the saddest sentence in the chapter: he wist not that the LORD was departed from him."},
  {q:"What did the Philistines do to Samson after seizing him?", options:["They put out his eyes and bound him with fetters of brass","They cut off his hands","They cast him into a pit of lions","They sold him to the Egyptians"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:21",verse:"But the Philistines took him, and put out his eyes, and brought him down to Gaza, and bound him with fetters of brass; and he did grind in the prison house.",
   insight:"The eyes that had lusted after Philistine women were taken. His sin found him out in the very place he had sinned."},
  {q:"What work was Samson forced to do in the prison house?", options:["He ground in the prison house","He carried water for the lords","He forged weapons for their army","He tended their vineyards"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:21",verse:"But the Philistines took him, and put out his eyes, and brought him down to Gaza, and bound him with fetters of brass; and he did grind in the prison house.",
   insight:"The mighty judge was reduced to a beast of burden, turning a millstone in circles. Pride had brought him lower than any enemy could."},
  {q:"What had begun to happen to Samson in prison?", options:["The hair of his head began to grow again","His sight began to return","His fetters began to rust","His jailers began to pity him"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:22",verse:"Howbeit the hair of his head began to grow again after he was shaven.",
   insight:"A small line of hope. God was quietly preparing a second chance while Samson ground in the dark."},
  {q:"To which god's temple was Samson brought to make sport for the Philistines?", options:["Dagon","Baal","Ashtaroth","Molech"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:23",verse:"Then the lords of the Philistines gathered them together for to offer a great sacrifice unto Dagon their god, and to rejoice: for they said, Our god hath delivered Samson our enemy into our hand.",
   insight:"Dagon was the Philistines' chief god. They credited their false god with Samson's capture, setting up God's final answer."},
  {q:"How many people were on the roof of the temple watching Samson?", options:["About three thousand men and women","About five hundred","About ten thousand","About one hundred"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:27",verse:"Now the house was full of men and women; and all the lords of the Philistines were there; and there were upon the roof about three thousand men and women, that beheld while Samson made sport.",
   insight:"The whole Philistine leadership gathered to laugh at him. Their celebration became the scene of their destruction."},
  {q:"What did Samson pray for in his final prayer?", options:["That God would remember him and strengthen him only this once","That God would restore his sight","That God would send fire from heaven","That God would forgive Delilah"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:28",verse:"And Samson called unto the LORD, and said, O Lord GOD, remember me, I pray thee, and strengthen me, I pray thee, only this once, O God, that I may be at once avenged of the Philistines for my two eyes.",
   insight:"Broken and blind, he finally prayed rightly. He asked not for himself but to be remembered, and God heard him."},
  {q:"How did Samson die?", options:["He bowed with all his might and pulled down the pillars, and the house fell upon the lords and all the people","He was slain by the Philistine lords with swords","He was crushed under a millstone","He died of hunger in the prison"], a:0, ref:"Judges 16:30",verse:"And Samson said, Let me die with the Philistines. And he bowed himself with all his might; and the house fell upon the lords, and upon all the people that were therein. So the dead which he slew at his death were more than they which he slew in his life.",
   insight:"In death he accomplished more than in life. God's strength returned for one final act of judgment on His enemies."}
 ]},
{n:17, name:"Micah's Idols", seal:{ref:"Judges 17:6",verse:"In those days there was no king in Israel, but every man did that which was right in his own eyes."},
 facts:["Did you know the refrain of these chapters, 'there was no king in Israel,' is the Bible's own diagnosis of the moral chaos that follows?", "Did you know Micah's mother dedicated stolen silver to the LORD and then used it to make a forbidden graven image?", "Did you know a Levite, who should have taught God's law, hired himself out as a private priest to Micah's household gods for ten shekels a year?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Who stole eleven hundred shekels of silver from his mother?", options:["Micah","The Levite of Bethlehem","Gideon","Abimelech"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:2",verse:"And he said unto his mother, The eleven hundred shekels of silver that were taken from thee, about which thou cursedst, and spakest of also in mine ears, behold, the silver is with me; I took it. And his mother said, Blessed be thou of the LORD, my son.",
   insight:"The chapter opens with theft inside a family, a picture of a household where God's commandments were already ignored."},
  {q:"What did Micah's mother do when he confessed and restored the silver?", options:["She blessed him in the name of the LORD","She cursed him","She cast him out of the house","She told the elders"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:2",verse:"And he said unto his mother, The eleven hundred shekels of silver that were taken from thee, about which thou cursedst, and spakest of also in mine ears, behold, the silver is with me; I took it. And his mother said, Blessed be thou of the LORD, my son.",
   insight:"Her blessing sounds pious, but what she does next with the silver shows her religion was mixed with idolatry."},
  {q:"What did Micah's mother say she had dedicated the silver to?", options:["Unto the LORD for her son, to make a graven image","Unto the poor of Ephraim","Unto the tabernacle at Shiloh","Unto the building of an altar"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:3",verse:"And when he had restored the eleven hundred shekels of silver to his mother, his mother said, I had wholly dedicated the silver unto the LORD from my hand for my son, to make a graven image and a molten image: now therefore I will restore it unto thee.",
   insight:"She used the LORD's name while breaking His commandment. Pious words cannot sanctify a forbidden act."},
  {q:"What was made from the silver?", options:["A graven image and a molten image","A golden calf","A brazen altar","A crown for Micah"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:4",verse:"Yet he restored the money unto his mother; and his mother took two hundred shekels of silver, and gave them to the founder, who made thereof a graven image and a molten image: and they were in the house of Micah.",
   insight:"A founder cast the idol from the dedicated silver. Israel was already copying the nations around her."},
  {q:"Besides the images, what did Micah have in his house of gods?", options:["An ephod and teraphim","A golden candlestick","The ark of the covenant","A table of shewbread"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:5",verse:"And the man Micah had an house of gods, and made an ephod, and teraphim, and consecrated one of his sons, who became his priest.",
   insight:"He was assembling a counterfeit religion piece by piece, copying the tabernacle's furnishings for his own shrine."},
  {q:"Whom did Micah consecrate as his priest at first?", options:["One of his sons","The Levite of Bethlehem","An elder of Ephraim","A man of Dan"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:5",verse:"And the man Micah had an house of gods, and made an ephod, and teraphim, and consecrated one of his sons, who became his priest.",
   insight:"Only Aaron's sons could be priests, yet Micah ordained his own son. He treated God's worship as his personal property."},
  {q:"What is the sad refrain given at the end of verse 6?", options:["In those days there was no king in Israel, but every man did that which was right in his own eyes","In those days Israel had rest from war forty years","In those days the word of the LORD was precious","In those days there was no open vision"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:6",verse:"In those days there was no king in Israel, but every man did that which was right in his own eyes.",
   insight:"This is the book's diagnosis of the age. Without God's king, everyone became their own law."},
  {q:"From which town did the young Levite come?", options:["Bethlehemjudah","Shiloh","Bethel","Hebron"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:7",verse:"And there was a young man out of Bethlehemjudah of the family of Judah, who was a Levite, and he sojourned there.",
   insight:"He was a Levite, set apart to serve God, yet he left his calling and wandered looking for a better offer."},
  {q:"Where was the young man sojourning when Micah met him?", options:["Mount Ephraim","The valley of Sorek","Gibeah of Benjamin","The hill country of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:8",verse:"And the man departed out of the city from Bethlehemjudah to sojourn where he could find a place: and he came to mount Ephraim to the house of Micah, as he journeyed.",
   insight:"He was drifting through Ephraim looking for a place to settle. A wandering minister is easy prey for a rich man's offer."},
  {q:"What did the young man say when Micah asked where he came from?", options:["I am a Levite of Bethlehemjudah, and I go to sojourn where I may find a place","I am a priest of the LORD at Shiloh","I am a prophet sent from God","I am a merchant seeking silver"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:9",verse:"And Micah said unto him, Whence comest thou? And he said unto him, I am a Levite of Bethlehemjudah, and I go to sojourn where I may find a place.",
   insight:"He identified himself by his tribe and his wandering. His holy office had become a resume for hire."},
  {q:"What did Micah ask the Levite to be for him?", options:["A father and a priest","A son and a servant","A watchman and a guard","A scribe and a teacher"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:10",verse:"And Micah said unto him, Dwell with me, and be unto me a father and a priest, and I will give thee ten shekels of silver by the year, and a suit of apparel, and thy victuals. So the Levite went in.",
   insight:"Micah wanted a personal chaplain to bless his idols. Religion had become a hired service rather than obedience."},
  {q:"What yearly wage did Micah offer the Levite?", options:["Ten shekels of silver, a suit of apparel, and his victuals","Thirty pieces of silver","A hundred shekels and a flock","Eleven hundred shekels of silver"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:10",verse:"And Micah said unto him, Dwell with me, and be unto me a father and a priest, and I will give thee ten shekels of silver by the year, and a suit of apparel, and thy victuals. So the Levite went in.",
   insight:"Ten shekels a year bought God's minister. Ministry sold for a price is already corrupted."},
  {q:"How did the Levite respond to Micah's offer?", options:["He was content to dwell with the man","He refused and returned to Bethlehem","He demanded twice the wage","He asked to consult the LORD first"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:11",verse:"And the Levite was content to dwell with the man; and the young man was unto him as one of his sons.",
   insight:"He was content. No struggle, no prayer, just quiet acceptance of a comfortable compromise."},
  {q:"What did the young man become to Micah?", options:["As one of his sons","As his hired servant only","As his enemy","As his brother"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:11",verse:"And the Levite was content to dwell with the man; and the young man was unto him as one of his sons.",
   insight:"Micah folded the Levite into his family business. The holy office was domesticated into household furniture."},
  {q:"Who consecrated the Levite as priest?", options:["Micah","The elders of Ephraim","The high priest at Shiloh","The young man consecrated himself"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:12",verse:"And Micah consecrated the Levite; and the young man became his priest, and was in the house of Micah.",
   insight:"Micah did it himself, as if priesthood were his to give. True consecration comes from God, not from an employer."},
  {q:"What made Micah sure the LORD would do him good?", options:["Because he had a Levite as his priest","Because he had restored the silver","Because he kept the feasts","Because he had built the images"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:13",verse:"Then said Micah, Now know I that the LORD will do me good, seeing I have a Levite to my priest.",
   insight:"He confused having a Levite with having God's favor. Outward religion without obedience is empty confidence."},
  {q:"In which mount did Micah have his house?", options:["Mount Ephraim","Mount Hermon","Mount Ebal","Mount Gerizim"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:1",verse:"And there was a man of mount Ephraim, whose name was Micah.",
   insight:"Ephraim was central Israel, so this corruption was not on the border but in the heart of the land."},
  {q:"How many shekels of silver did Micah's mother give to the founder?", options:["Two hundred shekels","Eleven hundred shekels","Ten shekels","Fifty shekels"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:4",verse:"Yet he restored the money unto his mother; and his mother took two hundred shekels of silver, and gave them to the founder, who made thereof a graven image and a molten image: and they were in the house of Micah.",
   insight:"She kept most of the silver and spent two hundred on the idol. Even her 'dedication' was half-hearted."},
  {q:"What did Micah conclude after securing a Levite as his priest?", options:["That the LORD would do him good","That his silver was cursed","That he must leave Ephraim","That the Danites would come"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:13",verse:"Then said Micah, Now know I that the LORD will do me good, seeing I have a Levite to my priest.", insight:"Micah mistook religious props for God's favor; having a priest never guaranteed having God."},
  {q:"Why could every man do what was right in his own eyes?", options:["Because there was no king in Israel","Because the judges had all died","Because the Philistines ruled the land","Because the law had been lost"], a:0, ref:"Judges 17:6",verse:"In those days there was no king in Israel, but every man did that which was right in his own eyes.",
   insight:"The verse ties moral anarchy to the absence of God's appointed order. People need God's rule, not their own."}
 ]},
{n:18, name:"The Danites Take Laish", seal:{ref:"Judges 18:29",verse:"And they called the name of the city Dan, after the name of Dan their father, who was born unto Israel: howbeit the name of the city was Laish at the first."},
 facts:["Did you know the tribe of Dan stole Micah's idols and his priest, then set up Israel's first rival worship center at Dan?", "Did you know the city the Danites renamed Dan was originally called Laish, a peaceful Sidonian town far from any help?", "Did you know Jonathan, the Levite who became Dan's priest, was the grandson of Moses himself?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Why did the tribe of Dan seek an inheritance to dwell in?", options:["Because their inheritance had not fallen unto them among the tribes","Because the Philistines had driven them from Judah","Because they wanted to live near the sea","Because Moses had promised them Laish"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:1",verse:"In those days there was no king in Israel: and in those days the tribe of the Danites sought them an inheritance to dwell in; for unto that day all their inheritance had not fallen unto them among the tribes of Israel.",
   insight:"Dan had failed to take its allotted land, so instead of trusting God they went looking for easier pickings."},
  {q:"How many men did the children of Dan send to spy out the land?", options:["Five men of valour","Twelve men, one from each tribe","Forty men","Two spies"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:2",verse:"And the children of Dan sent of their family five men from their coasts, men of valour, from Zorah, and from Eshtaol, to spy out the land, and to search it; and they said unto them, Go, search the land: who when they came to mount Ephraim, to the house of Micah, they lodged there.",
   insight:"Five spies went out from Dan's camp. Their mission began with theft in mind, not with seeking God's will."},
  {q:"From which two places did the five spies come?", options:["Zorah and Eshtaol","Timnath and Ekron","Gaza and Ashkelon","Hebron and Beersheba"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:2",verse:"And the children of Dan sent of their family five men from their coasts, men of valour, from Zorah, and from Eshtaol, to spy out the land, and to search it; and they said unto them, Go, search the land: who when they came to mount Ephraim, to the house of Micah, they lodged there.",
   insight:"Zorah and Eshtaol were Samson's home country. The tribe that produced Israel's strong man now produced thieves."},
  {q:"What did the spies ask the Levite to do for them?", options:["Ask counsel of God about their journey","Bless their swords","Join them as a spy","Give them Micah's silver"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:5",verse:"And they said unto him, Ask counsel, we pray thee, of God, that we may know whether our way which we go shall be prosperous.", insight:"They wanted God's counsel while serving idols; asking God to bless a crooked errand never works."},
  {q:"How did the spies recognize the young Levite?", options:["They knew the voice of the young man the Levite","They saw his priestly garments","Micah introduced him","He bore the ark"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:3",verse:"When they were by the house of Micah, they knew the voice of the young man the Levite: and they turned in thither, and said unto him, Who brought thee hither? and what makest thou in this place? and what hast thou here?",
   insight:"His voice gave him away. Even among strangers, a Levite was known, which made his compromise worse."},
  {q:"What did the spies ask the Levite to do for them?", options:["Ask counsel of God, that they might know whether their way would be prosperous","Offer a sacrifice for them","Bless their swords","Give them the images"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:5",verse:"And they said unto him, Ask counsel, we pray thee, of God, that we may know whether our way which we go shall be prosperous.",
   insight:"They wanted God's blessing on a plan of theft. People still ask God to bless what He has forbidden."},
  {q:"What answer did the Levite give the spies?", options:["Go in peace: before the LORD is your way wherein ye go","Return to your tribe and repent","The LORD forbids this journey","Wait seven days and I will tell you"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:6",verse:"And the priest said unto them, Go in peace: before the LORD is your way wherein ye go.",
   insight:"He spoke peace where there was no peace. A hired priest tells people what they want to hear."},
  {q:"What city did the spies find, dwelling careless and quiet?", options:["Laish","Jericho","Ai","Hazorr"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:7",verse:"Then the five men departed, and came to Laish, and saw the people that were therein, how they dwelt careless, after the manner of the Zidonians, quiet and secure; and there was no magistrate in the land, that might put them to shame in any thing; and they were far from the Zidonians, and had no business with any man.",
   insight:"Laish lived at ease, far from help. The Danites chose victims who could not fight back, not battles God commanded."},
  {q:"To which people were the people of Laish related, and how far was their help?", options:["They were Sidonians, and Sidon was far from them","They were Hittites, and Hebron was far from them","They were Amorites, and Jerusalem was far from them","They were Jebusites, and Jebus was far from them"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:7",verse:"Then the five men departed, and came to Laish, and saw the people that were therein, how they dwelt careless, after the manner of the Zidonians, quiet and secure; and there was no magistrate in the land, that might put them to shame in any thing; and they were far from the Zidonians, and had no business with any man.",
   insight:"Laish had no magistrate and no allies nearby. Their isolation, which they trusted, became their ruin."},
  {q:"What did the spies tell their brethren to do?", options:["Arise, that we may go up against them, for the land is very good","Make peace with Laish and trade with them","Wait for the LORD to give a sign","Return to Zorah and stay there"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:9",verse:"And they said, Arise, that we may go up against them: for we have seen the land, and, behold, it is very good: and are ye still? be not slothful to go, and to enter to possess the land.",
   insight:"They urged immediate attack on a peaceful people. Covetousness dressed itself up as opportunity."},
  {q:"How many armed men of Dan later went up from Zorah and Eshtaol?", options:["Six hundred men appointed with weapons of war","Five thousand men","Three hundred men","A thousand men"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:11",verse:"And there went from thence of the family of the Danites, out of Zorah and out of Eshtaol, six hundred men appointed with weapons of war.",
   insight:"Six hundred armed men marched north. This was no migration but an invasion."},
  {q:"Where did the six hundred camp on their way, giving the place a name?", options:["Kirjathjearim, called Mahanehdan","Bethel","Gibeah","Shiloh"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:12",verse:"And they went up, and pitched in Kirjath-jearim, in Judah: wherefore they called that place Mahaneh-dan unto this day: behold, it is behind Kirjath-jearim.",
   insight:"Mahanehdan means camp of Dan. They left a named trail of their expedition across Judah's territory."},
  {q:"What did the five spies tell the six hundred about Micah's house?", options:["That there was an ephod, teraphim, a graven image, and a molten image","That there was gold and silver to plunder","That there was a well of water","That there were armed guards"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:14",verse:"Then answered the five men that went to spy out the country of Laish, and said unto their brethren, Do ye know that there is in these houses an ephod, and teraphim, and a graven image, and a molten image? now therefore consider what ye have to do.",
   insight:"They remembered the idols from their first visit. The theft had been planned since the spying trip."},
  {q:"What did the Danites say to the Levite to win him over?", options:["Hold thy peace, lay thine hand upon thy mouth, and be a priest unto a tribe rather than one man's house","Stay and we will double your wage","Flee with us or die","Come and carry the images for us"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:19",verse:"And they said unto him, Hold thy peace, lay thine hand upon thy mouth, and go with us, and be to us a father and a priest: is it better for thee to be a priest unto the house of one man, or that thou be a priest unto a tribe and a family in Israel?",
   insight:"They offered him a promotion, from one man's priest to a tribe's priest. Ambition made his betrayal easy."},
  {q:"How did the Levite's heart respond?", options:["His heart was glad, and he took the ephod, teraphim, and graven image","His heart was heavy and he wept","His heart was afraid and he fled","His heart was angry and he cursed them"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:20",verse:"And the priest’s heart was glad, and he took the ephod, and the teraphim, and the graven image, and went in the midst of the people.",
   insight:"He was glad. No loyalty, no conscience, only delight at a better offer. That gladness condemns him."},
  {q:"When Micah pursued them, what did the Danites answer his cry?", options:["Let not thy voice be heard among us, lest angry fellows run upon thee","We will pay thee double for the images","The LORD commanded us to take them","Thy priest came willingly, so be still"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:25",verse:"And the children of Dan said unto him, Let not thy voice be heard among us, lest angry fellows run upon thee, and thou lose thy life, with the lives of thy household.",
   insight:"They answered a grieving man with threats. Might had become their only argument."},
  {q:"Why did Micah turn back home?", options:["He saw that they were too strong for him","His servants deserted him","The Levite begged him to stop","God spoke to him in a dream"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:26",verse:"And the children of Dan went their way: and when Micah saw that they were too strong for him, he turned and went back unto his house.",
   insight:"He was outnumbered and outarmed. The man who stole worship found he could not keep it by force."},
  {q:"What did the Danites do to Laish?", options:["They smote them with the edge of the sword and burnt the city with fire","They made a covenant with them","They besieged it for seven years","They bought it with silver"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:27",verse:"And they took the things which Micah had made, and the priest which he had, and came unto Laish, unto a people that were at quiet and secure: and they smote them with the edge of the sword, and burnt the city with fire.",
   insight:"They destroyed a peaceful city that had done them no harm. This was conquest without God's command."},
  {q:"What did the Danites call the rebuilt city?", options:["Dan, after the name of Dan their father","Mahanehdan","Laishjudah","Sidon"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:29",verse:"And they called the name of the city Dan, after the name of Dan their father, who was born unto Israel: howbeit the name of the city was Laish at the first.",
   insight:"They renamed it for their ancestor, stamping a stolen city with the family name."},
  {q:"Who became priest to the tribe of Dan, and whose son was he?", options:["Jonathan the son of Gershom, the son of Manasseh","Micah the son of Ephraim","Phinehas the son of Eleazar","Eli the son of Ithamar"], a:0, ref:"Judges 18:30",verse:"And the children of Dan set up the graven image: and Jonathan, the son of Gershom, the son of Manasseh, he and his sons were priests to the tribe of Dan until the day of the captivity of the land.",
   insight:"Jonathan was Moses' own grandson. The lawgiver's descendant led Israel's first organized idolatry, a chilling fall."}
 ]},
{n:19, name:"The Outrage at Gibeah", seal:{ref:"Judges 19:30",verse:"And it was so, that all that saw it said, There was no such deed done nor seen from the day that the children of Israel came up out of the land of Egypt unto this day: consider of it, take advice, and speak your minds."},
 facts:["Did you know the horror at Gibeah so shocked Israel that the tribes said nothing like it had happened since they left Egypt?", "Did you know the old man who sheltered the Levite was himself a sojourner from Mount Ephraim, a stranger showing hospitality in a town that had none?", "Did you know the Levite's grim message, a concubine divided into twelve pieces, summoned all Israel to Mizpeh and sparked a civil war?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"What was the condition of Israel when this story happened?", options:["There was no king in Israel","Saul reigned over Israel","David ruled in Hebron","The Philistines oppressed Israel"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:1",verse:"And it came to pass in those days, when there was no king in Israel, that there was a certain Levite sojourning on the side of mount Ephraim, who took to him a concubine out of Bethlehemjudah.",
   insight:"The refrain returns. Without God's king, Israel sank to a depravity matching Sodom's."},
  {q:"Who took a concubine from Bethlehemjudah?", options:["A Levite sojourning on the side of mount Ephraim","Micah of mount Ephraim","A Benjamite of Gibeah","An elder of Mizpeh"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:1",verse:"And it came to pass in those days, when there was no king in Israel, that there was a certain Levite sojourning on the side of mount Ephraim, who took to him a concubine out of Bethlehemjudah.",
   insight:"A Levite, of all men, lived in open disregard of God's pattern for marriage. Corruption had reached the clergy."},
  {q:"Where did the concubine flee to?", options:["To her father's house at Bethlehemjudah","To the house of Micah","To the city of Jebus","To Mizpeh"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:2",verse:"And his concubine played the whore against him, and went away from him unto her father’s house to Bethlehemjudah, and was there four whole months.",
   insight:"She went home to her father. The Levite's household was already broken before the journey began."},
  {q:"How long was she at her father's house?", options:["Four months","Seven days","A full year","Forty days"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:2",verse:"And his concubine played the whore against him, and went away from him unto her father’s house to Bethlehemjudah, and was there four whole months.",
   insight:"Four months passed before he went after her. This was no urgent rescue but a delayed afterthought."},
  {q:"Who went with the Levite to fetch her?", options:["His servant and a couple of asses","His father and his brethren","Two men of Ephraim","The elders of his city"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:3",verse:"And her husband arose, and went after her, to speak friendly unto her, and to bring her again, having his servant with him, and a couple of asses: and she brought him into her father’s house: and when the father of the damsel saw him, he rejoiced to meet him.",
   insight:"He brought a servant and donkeys, expecting to carry her home. He had no idea what awaited them."},
  {q:"How did the damsel's father receive the Levite?", options:["He rejoiced to meet him","He shut the door against him","He demanded a dowry","He sent him away empty"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:3",verse:"And her husband arose, and went after her, to speak friendly unto her, and to bring her again, having his servant with him, and a couple of asses: and she brought him into her father’s house: and when the father of the damsel saw him, he rejoiced to meet him.",
   insight:"The father was glad to see him. That warm welcome makes the coming tragedy even darker."},
  {q:"How many days did the father keep the Levite eating and drinking?", options:["Five days, for he retained him repeatedly","One day only","Seven days","Three days"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:8",verse:"And he arose early in the morning on the fifth day to depart: and the damsel’s father said, Comfort thine heart, I pray thee. And they tarried until afternoon, and they did eat both of them.",
   insight:"The father kept delaying their departure with hospitality. Those lost hours pushed their journey into the night."},
  {q:"What time of day did they finally leave Bethlehem?", options:["The day grew toward evening","At the break of dawn","At high noon","At midnight"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:9",verse:"And when the man rose up to depart, he, and his concubine, and his servant, his father in law, the damsel’s father, said unto him, Behold, now the day draweth toward evening, I pray you tarry all night: behold, the day groweth to an end, lodge here, that thine heart may be merry; and to morrow get you early on your way, that thou mayest go home.",
   insight:"They left late because of lingering at the table. Small delays can lead to terrible destinations."},
  {q:"Which city did the servant suggest they lodge in?", options:["Jebus, the city of the Jebusites","Gibeah of Benjamin","Ramah","Bethel"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:11",verse:"And when they were by Jebus, the day was far spent; and the servant said unto his master, Come, I pray thee, and let us turn in into this city of the Jebusites, and lodge in it.",
   insight:"Jebus was a foreign city, yet the Levite refused it. He chose an Israelite town, expecting safety among his own people."},
  {q:"Why did the Levite refuse to turn aside into Jebus?", options:["Because it was a city of a stranger, not of the children of Israel","Because the gates were shut","Because he had no money for an inn","Because the Jebusites were at war"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:12",verse:"And his master said unto him, We will not turn aside hither into the city of a stranger, that is not of the children of Israel; we will pass over to Gibeah.",
   insight:"He trusted Israelites over strangers. The story's horror is that his own countrymen proved worse than heathen."},
  {q:"In which city did they lodge instead?", options:["Gibeah, which belongeth to Benjamin","Ramah of Benjamin","Mizpeh","Gibeon"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:14",verse:"And they passed on and went their way; and the sun went down upon them when they were by Gibeah, which belongeth to Benjamin.",
   insight:"Gibeah belonged to Benjamin, Saul's future tribe. Its wickedness would nearly wipe the tribe out."},
  {q:"Where did they sit down when no one took them in?", options:["In the street of the city","At the city gate","By the well outside the walls","In the marketplace"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:15",verse:"And they turned aside thither, to go in and to lodge in Gibeah: and when he went in, he sat him down in a street of the city: for there was no man that took them into his house to lodging.",
   insight:"No one offered lodging in an Israelite city. The lack of hospitality was the first warning sign."},
  {q:"Who finally took them into his house?", options:["An old man from mount Ephraim sojourning in Gibeah","A Benjamite elder","The mayor of Gibeah","A young shepherd"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:16",verse:"And, behold, there came an old man from his work out of the field at even, which was also of mount Ephraim; and he sojourned in Gibeah: but the men of the place were Benjamites.",
   insight:"A fellow sojourner showed the kindness the townsmen withheld. Strangers often shame settled people."},
  {q:"What did the old man say to the Levite about lodging in the street?", options:["Only lodge not in the street","Pay me silver for the night","Leave thy concubine with me","Go back to Jebus"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:20",verse:"And the old man said, Peace be with thee; howsoever let all thy wants lie upon me; only lodge not in the street.",
   insight:"He knew Gibeah's streets were unsafe. His warning shows the town's evil was no secret."},
  {q:"Who beset the old man's house at night?", options:["Certain sons of Belial of the city","The Philistines","Robbers from the hills","The men of Jebus"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:22",verse:"Now as they were making their hearts merry, behold, the men of the city, certain sons of Belial, beset the house round about, and beat at the door, and spake to the master of the house, the old man, saying, Bring forth the man that came into thine house, that we may know him.",
   insight:"Sons of Belial means worthless, lawless men. Gibeah had become a second Sodom."},
  {q:"What wicked demand did they make?", options:["Bring forth the man that came into thine house, that we may know him","Give us thy silver and thy asses","Deliver thy concubine to us","Open thy house that we may plunder it"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:22",verse:"Now as they were making their hearts merry, behold, the men of the city, certain sons of Belial, beset the house round about, and beat at the door, and spake to the master of the house, the old man, saying, Bring forth the man that came into thine house, that we may know him.",
   insight:"Their demand matches the men of Sodom at Lot's door. Israel had sunk to the level of the cities God destroyed."},
  {q:"Whom did the Levite thrust out to the men instead?", options:["His concubine","His servant","The old man's daughter","Himself"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:25",verse:"But the men would not hearken to him: so the man took his concubine, and brought her forth unto them; and they knew her, and abused her all the night until the morning: and when the day began to spring, they let her go.",
   insight:"He sacrificed her to save himself. His cowardice is one of the chapter's darkest stains."},
  {q:"Where was the woman found in the morning?", options:["Fallen down at the door of the man's house, with her hands upon the threshold","Sleeping in the courtyard","Fled to the city gate","Lying in the street"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:27",verse:"And her lord rose up in the morning, and opened the doors of the house, and went out to go his way: and, behold, the woman his concubine was fallen down at the door of the house, and her hands were upon the threshold.",
   insight:"Her hands on the threshold tell the whole tragedy. She crawled home and died reaching for safety."},
  {q:"What did the Levite do when he found her dead?", options:["He divided her into twelve pieces and sent her throughout all the coasts of Israel","He buried her at Gibeah","He carried her back to Bethlehem","He wept and returned to Ephraim"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:29",verse:"And when he was come into his house, he took a knife, and laid hold on his concubine, and divided her, together with her bones, into twelve pieces, and sent her into all the coasts of Israel.",
   insight:"His gruesome message shocked the nation awake. Sometimes only horror can rouse a sleeping conscience."},
  {q:"What did all Israel say when they heard of it?", options:["There was no such deed done nor seen from the day Israel came up from Egypt unto this day","This is the work of the Philistines","Let us forget it and keep the peace","The Levite himself is to blame"], a:0, ref:"Judges 19:30",verse:"And it was so, that all that saw it said, There was no such deed done nor seen from the day that the children of Israel came up out of the land of Egypt unto this day: consider of it, take advice, and speak your minds.",
   insight:"They recognized this as unprecedented evil. The outrage united Israel as nothing else could."}
 ]},
{n:20, name:"War Against Benjamin", seal:{ref:"Judges 20:1",verse:"Then all the children of Israel went out, and the congregation was gathered together as one man, from Dan even to Beer-sheba, with the land of Gilead, unto the LORD in Mizpeh."},
 facts:["Did you know all Israel gathered 'as one man' at Mizpeh, from Dan to Beersheba, the largest assembly in the book of Judges?", "Did you know Israel lost the first two battles against Benjamin, forty thousand men, before God gave them victory?", "Did you know only six hundred Benjamites survived the civil war, hiding four months in the rock of Rimmon?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Where did all the children of Israel assemble as one man?", options:["Mizpeh","Shiloh","Bethel","Gilgal"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:1",verse:"Then all the children of Israel went out, and the congregation was gathered together as one man, from Dan even to Beer-sheba, with the land of Gilead, unto the LORD in Mizpeh.",
   insight:"Mizpeh became the gathering place of a united Israel. The horror at Gibeah accomplished what judges had not."},
  {q:"How many men of Israel drew the sword at the assembly?", options:["Four hundred thousand footmen","Six hundred thousand","Thirty thousand","Seventy thousand"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:2",verse:"And the chief of all the people, even of all the tribes of Israel, presented themselves in the assembly of the people of God, four hundred thousand footmen that drew sword.",
   insight:"Four hundred thousand armed men answered the call. All Israel stood together against one tribe's sin."},
  {q:"Which tribe heard that Israel had gone up to Mizpeh?", options:["The children of Benjamin","The children of Dan","The children of Judah","The children of Ephraim"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:3",verse:"(Now the children of Benjamin heard that the children of Israel were gone up to Mizpeh.) Then said the children of Israel, Tell us, how was this wickedness?",
   insight:"Benjamin heard and prepared for war instead of surrendering the guilty men. Tribal pride chose battle over justice."},
  {q:"What did the Levite tell the assembly the men of Gibeah had done?", options:["They beset the house and thought to have slain him, and his concubine they forced till she died","They stole his silver","They burned his house","They took him captive"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:5",verse:"And the men of Gibeah rose against me, and beset the house round about upon me by night, and thought to have slain me: and my concubine have they forced, that she is dead.",
   insight:"He told his version, leaving out his own cowardice. Even so, the crime he described demanded justice."},
  {q:"What did all the people answer with one voice?", options:["We will not any of us go to his tent, neither will we any of us turn into his house, till we have done this thing against Gibeah","We will forgive Benjamin","We will ask the king to decide","We will go home and think on it"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:8",verse:"And all the people arose as one man, saying, We will not any of us go to his tent, neither will we any of us turn into his house.",
   insight:"They bound themselves with an oath before acting. Solemn purpose marked this assembly."},
  {q:"How did Israel decide who would go up against Gibeah first?", options:["By lot, taking ten men of an hundred for victuals","By the Urim and Thummim alone","By choosing volunteers","By the eldest tribe going first"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:9",verse:"But now this shall be the thing which we will do to Gibeah; we will go up by lot against it;",
   insight:"They organized carefully, setting aside supply men. Even righteous anger needs order."},
  {q:"What did Israel demand of Benjamin before the battle?", options:["Deliver the children of Belial which are in Gibeah, that we may put them to death","Pay a thousand shekels of silver","Surrender the city of Gibeah","Give up the old man of Ephraim"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:13",verse:"Now therefore deliver us the men, the children of Belial, which are in Gibeah, that we may put them to death, and put away evil from Israel. But the children of Benjamin would not hearken to the voice of their brethren the children of Israel:",
   insight:"They asked only for the guilty men. This was justice, not conquest, and Benjamin refused it."},
  {q:"How did the children of Benjamin respond?", options:["They would not hearken to the voice of their brethren","They delivered the guilty men","They asked for seven days to decide","They fled to the rock Rimmon"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:13",verse:"Now therefore deliver us the men, the children of Belial, which are in Gibeah, that we may put them to death, and put away evil from Israel. But the children of Benjamin would not hearken to the voice of their brethren the children of Israel:",
   insight:"They chose tribal loyalty over righteousness. Protecting sinners brought ruin on the whole tribe."},
  {q:"How many Benjamites were numbered for the war?", options:["Twenty and six thousand men that drew sword, besides the inhabitants of Gibeah","Four hundred thousand","Six hundred men","Seventy thousand"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:15",verse:"And the children of Benjamin were numbered at that time out of the cities twenty and six thousand men that drew sword, beside the inhabitants of Gibeah, which were numbered seven hundred chosen men.",
   insight:"Twenty-six thousand stood against four hundred thousand. Their courage was real, but their cause was wrong."},
  {q:"What were the seven hundred chosen men of Benjamin famous for?", options:["They were lefthanded, and could sling stones at an hair breadth and not miss","They were giants","They could run like deer","They were skilled with the bow"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:16",verse:"Among all this people there were seven hundred chosen men lefthanded; every one could sling stones at an hair breadth, and not miss.",
   insight:"Their skill was remarkable, every stone finding its mark. Talent in a bad cause only deepens the tragedy."},
  {q:"Whom did Israel ask God about going up to battle, and what was the answer?", options:["Which of us shall go up first, and the LORD said, Judah shall go up first","Shall we go at all, and the LORD said no","Who is guilty, and the LORD named Gibeah","How many shall we send, and the LORD said ten thousand"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:18",verse:"And the children of Israel arose, and went up to the house of God, and asked counsel of God, and said, Which of us shall go up first to the battle against the children of Benjamin? And the LORD said, Judah shall go up first.",
   insight:"God answered their question but did not promise victory. Seeking God is right even when the outcome is hidden."},
  {q:"What happened on the first day of battle?", options:["Benjamin destroyed twenty and two thousand men of Israel","Israel destroyed Benjamin","Both armies fled","God sent hailstones on Benjamin"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:21",verse:"And the children of Benjamin came forth out of Gibeah, and destroyed down to the ground of the Israelites that day twenty and two thousand men.",
   insight:"Twenty-two thousand Israelites fell on day one. God let His own people suffer to humble their confidence."},
  {q:"What did Israel do after the first defeat?", options:["They wept before the LORD until even, and asked, Shall I go up again","They blamed Judah","They offered sacrifices to Baal","They returned home"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:23",verse:"(And the children of Israel went up and wept before the LORD until even, and asked counsel of the LORD, saying, Shall I go up again to battle against the children of Benjamin my brother? And the LORD said, Go up against him.)",
   insight:"They wept and asked again. Defeat drove them to prayer, which is often God's purpose in it."},
  {q:"What happened on the second day of battle?", options:["Benjamin destroyed eighteen thousand more men of Israel","Israel broke through to Gibeah","The battle was stopped by rain","Benjamin surrendered"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:25",verse:"And Benjamin went forth against them out of Gibeah the second day, and destroyed down to the ground of the children of Israel again eighteen thousand men; all these drew the sword.",
   insight:"Another eighteen thousand fell. God was teaching Israel that numbers and zeal cannot replace His timing."},
  {q:"Where did Israel go to weep and sacrifice after the second defeat?", options:["To the house of God, and offered burnt offerings and peace offerings","To Mizpeh","To Gilgal","To the rock Rimmon"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:26",verse:"Then all the children of Israel, and all the people, went up, and came unto the house of God, and wept, and sat there before the LORD, and fasted that day until even, and offered burnt offerings and peace offerings before the LORD.",
   insight:"This time they added fasting and sacrifice to their weeping. Their repentance deepened with each loss."},
  {q:"Who was priest, standing before the ark in those days, whom they inquired of?", options:["Phinehas the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron","Eli the priest","Jonathan the son of Gershom","Abiathar"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:28",verse:"And Phinehas, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron, stood before it in those days,) saying, Shall I yet again go out to battle against the children of Benjamin my brother, or shall I cease? And the LORD said, Go up; for to morrow I will deliver them into thine hand.",
   insight:"Phinehas, the zealous priest of Moses' day, still ministered. God's covenant presence had never left Israel."},
  {q:"What promise did the LORD give Israel the third time?", options:["Go up, for to morrow I will deliver them into thine hand","Flee before Benjamin","Wait seven days","Make peace with Benjamin"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:28",verse:"And Phinehas, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron, stood before it in those days,) saying, Shall I yet again go out to battle against the children of Benjamin my brother, or shall I cease? And the LORD said, Go up; for to morrow I will deliver them into thine hand.",
   insight:"After two defeats and true humbling, God promised victory. His delays are not denials."},
  {q:"What strategy did Israel use on the third day?", options:["They set liers in wait round about Gibeah","They attacked at midnight","They built siege towers","They dammed the river"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:29",verse:"And Israel set liers in wait round about Gibeah.",
   insight:"The ambush echoed Joshua's victory at Ai. God gave the plan, and Israel finally obeyed it fully."},
  {q:"What sign did the ambush use to tell Israel to turn back on Benjamin?", options:["A great flame with smoke rising up out of the city","A trumpet blast","A banner on the hill","A shout from the valley"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:38",verse:"Now there was an appointed sign between the men of Israel and the liers in wait, that they should make a great flame with smoke rise up out of the city.",
   insight:"The burning city was the signal. Benjamin's triumph turned to terror when they saw the smoke."},
  {q:"How many men of Benjamin fell in the battle?", options:["Twenty and five thousand","Ten thousand","Twenty and two thousand","Eighteen thousand"], a:0, ref:"Judges 20:46",verse:"So that all which fell that day of Benjamin were twenty and five thousand men that drew the sword; all these were men of valour.",
   insight:"Twenty-five thousand Benjamites died, nearly the whole tribe. Sin defended to the end brought almost total ruin."}
 ]}
]);
